course: dictionary
lesson: 6235
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the dictionary lists them. To pick the right one, read the context: the other words in the sentence and the sentences around it. «Fans mobbed the singer as she left the stage.» Here mob is an action: the fans crowded round her in a big, excited group.
explain: The same word can also be a thing. «An angry mob gathered outside the town hall.» Now mob is a noun, a large, noisy crowd that may turn violent. Ask yourself: is this word a thing or an action? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Some meanings belong to certain places or ways of speaking. In Australia, «a mob of kangaroos» is a group of kangaroos, and some Aboriginal people say «my mob» to mean their family or community. In Britain, «the whole mob» can mean a gang of friends. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Mob has several meanings, so check the context before you decide which one is right.
fact: As a noun, «mob» means a large, noisy, often angry crowd that may become violent.
fact: As a verb, «mob» means to crowd round someone in a big, excited or aggressive group, as in «fans mobbed the singer».
fact: In Australia, «a mob of kangaroos» is a group of kangaroos or cattle, and «my mob» can mean a person's family or community.
fact: In a mob, people sometimes do things they would never do alone, so always think for yourself.
exercise: «Fans mobbed the singer as she left the stage.» What does mobbed mean here? | *Crowded round her in an excited group | Sang along with her quietly | Bought tickets for her next show | Ran away from her quickly
exercise: «An angry mob gathered outside the town hall.» What is a mob in this sentence? | A kind of town hall | *A large, noisy crowd that may turn violent | A quiet, sleepy animal | A small family who lives in one house
exercise: Meerkats live in «mobs of up to about 50». What is a mob of meerkats? | A meerkat standing guard on its own | *A group of meerkats living together | One meerkat who lives alone | A type of burrow
test: «Meerkats live in mobs of up to about 50, and a meerkat alone could not keep watch and feed at the same time.» What is a mob here? | A large, angry crowd | A place where meerkats sleep | *A group of meerkats living together | A meerkat who is lost
test: The text says people in a mob can act worse than they would alone. Why? | *Crowd feelings can make people do things they would never do alone | Being alone makes people braver | Crowds are always calm and kind | Mobs only form at football matches
exam: Which sentence uses mob as a verb? | «A mob of cattle grazed in the field» | *«The fans mobbed the players at the gate» | «The angry mob outside the shop shouted and pushed» | «The whole mob came round for pizza»
